    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d196">

                <pb facs="0347" n="175" xml:id="pg175"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.857/404</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at London</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Skinner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-03-14">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 14, 1917</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received 2.45 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>American Consul, Plymouth, reports British <hi rendition="#italic">East
                        Point</hi>, London for Philadelphia, torpedoed 9th, 5. p.m., about 15 miles
                    west of Eddystone Lighthouse. <hi rendition="#italic">East Point</hi> not
                    warned, carried one gun aft, armed patrol boat near by. Crew of 45 left <hi
                        rendition="#italic">East Point</hi> in four; boats, all saved.
                        John Webber of, 159 Fougeron Street, Buffalo, fireman,
                    and Edward Ellis, 565 Cortland Avenue, Brooklyn, seaman,
                    both American citizens, have made affidavits at Plymouth.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Skinner</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
